 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Event Planning Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is an event planning form?\", \"a\": \"An event planning form collects what a planner needs before booking anything: the date, expected headcount, budget, and venue needs. Organizations use it to approve events, and planners use it as a client intake.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I make an event planning form?\", \"a\": \"Decide what the answers feed, usually an approval or a quote, then ask only for what that step needs: the date, a headcount, and a budget split into lines. Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a> makes the form from a plain description, with the approval routing and dated task list behind it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What questions are on an event planning questionnaire?\", \"a\": \"Questions about taste and priorities, once the logistics are known. A planner's event planning questionnaire asks about the occasion and the feel the client wants. Then it asks them to rank food, the venue, music, and decor. That ranking decides where extra money goes and what gets trimmed first.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is an event planning survey used for?\", \"a\": \"Two jobs: shaping an event before it happens and judging how it went. Beforehand, an event planning survey asks likely guests which dates and ticket prices work for them. Afterward, a short <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/feedback'>feedback form</a> asks what worked and what to change. Emergent puts no cap on survey responses, however large the guest list.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is there a free event planning form template?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Template galleries such as Smartsheet offer free event planning templates, and some universities post their own for student groups. An event planning form template suits a one-off event. Emergent is free to start, and a form built there keeps every request as a record you can sort and total.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you track approvals on an event planning form?\", \"a\": \"Give every request a status, and record who signed off and when. A status column in a spreadsheet holds up for a handful of events. In an app, each decision is logged against the request, and the organizer can see which sign-off is still pending. The app Emergent builds has no per-approver seats: a facilities manager who signs off twice a year costs nothing extra.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a good deadline for an event planning form?\", \"a\": \"One set by the slowest step, not a round number. Add the longest vendor lead time to the time approvals take. If the venue books 60 days out and sign-offs take 14 days, the form is due 74 days before the event. Guest sign-ups open after approval, through a separate <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/registration'>registration form</a>.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should event planning forms be online or on paper?\", \"a\": \"Online, as soon as requests start to repeat. An event planning form PDF prints cleanly for a one-off event, but every answer has to be retyped before anyone can total a budget. Online, each answer is data the form can add up and route the moment it arrives.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between an event planning form and a questionnaire?\", \"a\": \"The form collects facts someone has to act on, like the date and headcount an approver or vendor needs. The questionnaire collects preferences, such as the mood and the must-haves. An organization approving events needs only the form. A planner pricing a client's event can send both, starting with the questionnaire.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should event planning forms include?\", \"a\": \"The date and times, expected headcount, a budget broken into lines and one contact person, plus food and AV needs. Event planning forms for public events add a promotion plan and a link to the <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder/event'>event landing page</a> guests will see.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
